hreshold."],"overallConfidence":0.57,"conflictingSignals":["Close match between Walking/Working Surfaces and Fall Protection."],"recommendedFollowup":["Add missing context before relying on final standard selection."],"missingCriticalInformation":["Employee exposure is not clearly described.","Specific location or equipment context is limited."],"supervisorReviewRecommended":true},"crossDomainInteraction":{"reviewFocus":[],"interactions":[],"escalationRisks":[],"interactionSummary":"No cross-domain interaction pattern detected from available domain intelligence.","interactionSeverity":"none"},"decisionExplainability":{"keyEvidence":["A physical condition appears degraded, missing, damaged, or uncontrolled. → The hazard appears accessible during normal work or travel. → Energy transfer mechanism requires confirmation. → Control failure mechanism requires confirmation. → Potential consequence requires supervisor review.","Applicable standards were identified.","1 applicable standard suggestion(s) identified."],"uncertainty":["employee exposure is not clearly described.","specific location or equipment context is limited.","close match between walking/working surfaces and fall protection.","worker exposure is not explicitly described."],"riskStatement":"Risk is rated Moderate.","decisionSummary":"Walking/Working Surfaces was selected based on hazard signals, context, risk indicators, and standards alignment.","controlStatement":"Durable controls are represented in the recommendation set.","escalationFactors":["Supervisor review recommended by confidence intelligence.","Control intelligence recommends verification before closure."],"standardsStatement":"Top standards were selected from curated HazLenz AI mappings and CFR-backed matching where available.","confidenceStatement":"Overall confidence is 57%.","supervisorReviewRecommended":true},"candidateStandardFamily":"machine_guarding","citationLevelCandidates":[],"correlationIntelligence":{"recommendation":"No strong hazard-correlation pattern detected from available information.","cascadePotential":"low","correlatedHazards":["Walking/Working Surfaces"],"correlatedSignals":[],"cascadingRiskPaths":[],"systemicIndicators":[],"escalationRecommended":false},"clarificationAnswerState":{"invalidAnswers":[],"answeredQuestionIds":[]},"exposurePathIntelligence":{"exposureSummary":"HazLenz AI mapped likely exposure pathways, worker interfaces, and amplification conditions contributing to hazard exposure.","exposurePathways":[],"workerInterfaces":[],"exposureAmplifiers":["Uncontrolled energy conditions may amplify injury severity."],"exposureComplexity":"low"},"multiHazardDecomposition":{"hazards":[{"domainId":"walking_working_surfaces","hazardId":"haz-1","mechanism":"floor","confidence":0.4,"evidenceGaps":[],"hazardFamily":"walking_working_surfaces","conditionState":"ACTIVE","correctionStatus":"not_stated","currentCondition":"Current exposure remains described.","temporalEvidence":[],"reviewerQuestions":[],"supportingSignals":["floor","hole"],"standardCandidates":[{"title":"Machine guarding - types of guarding methods","family":"OSHA General Industry machine guarding","status":"UNKNOWN","citation":"29 CFR 1910.212(a)(1)","sourceKey":"osha-ecfr-1910","confidence":0.45,"sourceName":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910 General Industry Standards","sourceType":"regulation","explanation":"Candidate only; missing: moving or accessible energy.","corpusBacked":false,"applicability":"candidate","backingStatus":"UNAPPROVED_CONTENT","contentDisclosure":"HAZLENZ_AUTHORED","missingPredicates":["moving or accessible energy"],"plainLanguageSummary":"One or more methods of machine guarding must be provided to protect the operator and other employees in the machine area from hazards such as those created by point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, and flying chips and sparks; the rule gives barrier guards, two-hand tripping devices and electronic safety devices as examples of guarding methods (29 CFR 1910.212(a)(1)). Guards must be affixed to the machine where possible and secured elsewhere if attachment to the machine is not possible, and the guard must not itself offer an accident hazard (1910.212(a)(2)). Point-of-operation guarding requirements are addressed separately at 1910.212(a)(3).","jurisdictionProvenance":"USER_CONFIRMED"},{"title":"Duty to have fall protection and falling object protection","family":"OSHA walking-working surface fall protection","status":"UNKNOWN","citation":"29 CFR 1910.28","sourceKey":"osha-ecfr-1910","confidence":0.45,"sourceName":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910 General Industry Standards","sourceType":"regulation","explanation":"Candidate only; missing: employee access or exposure.","corpusBacked":false,"applicability":"candidate","backingStatus":"UNAPPROVED_CONTENT","contentDisclosure":"HAZLENZ_AUTHORED","missingPredicates":["employee access or exposure"],"plainLanguageSummary":"Section 1910.28 requires general-industry employers to protect each employee exposed to fall and falling-object hazards on walking-working surfaces, and 1910.28(b) sets the protection required for each situation. For stairways, 1910.28(b)(11) requires that each employee exposed to an unprotected side or edge of a stairway landing 4 feet (1.2 m) or more above a lower level be protected by a guardrail or stair rail system (1910.28(b)(11)(i)), and that each flight of stairs having at least 3 treads and at least 4 risers be equipped with stair rail systems and handrails (1910.28(b)(11)(ii)) — so the number of treads and risers is part of establishing whether the handrail requirement applies to a particular flight. Ship stairs and alternating tread type stairs must have handrails on both sides (1910.28(b)(11)(iii)). Construction and dimensional requirements for stairways themselves are at 29 CFR 1910.25, and the criteria for stair rail systems and handrails are at 1910.29, not in this section. Fall protection for employees on scaffolds is governed by 29 CFR part 1926 subpart L (1910.28(b)(12)(i)).","jurisdictionProvenance":"USER_CONFIRMED"}],"observationFragment":"the unguarded pulley sits beside an open floor hole","possibleOverlapWith":[],"requiresHumanReview":false},{"domainId":"hazcom","hazardId":"haz-2","mechanism":"label","confidence":0.2,"evidenceGaps":[],"hazardFamily":"hazard_communication","conditionState":"ACTIVE","correctionStatus":"not_stated","temporalEvidence":[],"reviewerQuestions":[],"supportingSignals":["label"],"standardCandidates":[{"title":"Hazard communication","family":"OSHA hazard communication labeling","status":"SUPPORTED","citation":"29 CFR 1910.1200","sourceKey":"osha-ecfr-1910","confidence":0.96,"sourceName":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910 General Industry Standards","sourceType":"regulation","explanation":"Supported by submitted evidence for OSHA hazard communication labeling; qualified review remains required.","corpusBacked":false,"applicability":"direct","backingStatus":"UNAPPROVED_CONTENT","contentDisclosure":"HAZLENZ_AUTHORED","missingPredicates":[],"plainLanguageSummary":"Employers must ensure that each container of hazardous chemicals in the workplace is labeled, tagged or marked with either the full shipped-container information required by 29 CFR 1910.1200(f)(1)(i)-(v), or with the product identifier plus words, pictures or symbols giving at least general information about the chemical's hazards which, together with the other information available under the employer's hazard communication program, conveys the specific physical and health hazards (1910.1200(f)(6)). This workplace-labeling duty is distinct from 1910.1200(f)(1), which places the duty to label containers leaving the workplace on the chemical manufacturer, importer or distributor. Employers may use signs, placards, process sheets, batch tickets, operating procedures or other written materials in lieu of affixing labels to individual stationary process containers, provided the alternative identifies the containers and conveys the required information (1910.12